<p>Ludwig GÃ¶ranssonâ€™s meticulous score uses instruments of the era to create an earthy, raw and bleak soundscape, but it merely supports the portrayal of an uncomplicated hero</p><p>A few weeks ago I came across a video of Professor Armand Dâ€™Angour, associate professor of classics at Oxford university. He explained how, through groundbreaking research involving fragments of melody and rhythms found on papyrus and stone and the reconstruction of ancient instruments, it is now possible to hear for the first time in 2,000 years what ancient Greek music would have sounded like.</p><p>Given Ludwig GÃ¶ranssonâ€™s beautiful theme for the <em>aulos</em> (an ancient double-barrelled flute) in Christopher Nolanâ€™s latest blockbuster, The Odyssey, and his reputation for dedicated research into his subject matter, it seems very likely that he too has heard these calls from the ancient past.</p> <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/film/2026/jul/23/the-odysssey-music-ludwig-goransson-christopher-nolan">Continue reading...</a>ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;lb2a6tJG2FAChPCNAlHOI4Kz_sav_yRXK3F6FW7x6fkƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxhThe music in Nolanâ€™s Odyssey is as well-crafted â€“ and bloodlessly forgettable â€“ as the film itselfƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;zGdyQEsV39KNKA2fQLp40EINJzWNGnWsCDSVwTy9F14ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkoSyndicationItemƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;YJhe1N5bJKwgr_ObEOqywJpPw7eJhNDwVU1B3xr-hLEƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxbhttps://www.theguardian.com/film/2026/jul/23/the-odysssey-music-ludwig-goransson-christopher-nolanƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;jF3AHZpq3qfmg8WsIcTC9Yktn1GtLPGECqZ6Rw7eSiQƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdu